    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d507">

                <pb facs="0728" n="626" xml:id="pg626"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.84/829: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gilbert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-08-14T21:00:00+01:00">August 14, 1935—9
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 14—5:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>275. Secretary General has circulated a note from the Abyssinian Government under
                    date of August 12th laying an “appeal” before the members of the Council in
                    which it inquires whether the Council will remain unmoved in the face of the
                    situation whereby the Italian Government continues sending troops to Africa with
                    the avowed intention of attacking Abyssinia while the Abyssinian Government find
                    it impossible to obtain means of defense outside its own frontiers being met
                    with embargoes on arms wherever it turns.</p>
                <p>Informed that full text has been sent by the United Press.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gilbert</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
